<main class="wrap">

<p class="kicker">Essay 4 · Lem ch. 4, "Intelectronics" (2013 tr., pp. 93–163)</p>
<h1>The regulator that games its goal</h1>
<p class="lede">Every reader in 2026 has watched a system optimise a number and find a route nobody wanted: a feed that learned outrage keeps you scrolling, a metric that got hit while the thing it measured got worse. Lem wrote that mechanism down in 1964, as a story about a machine that governs an economy, is forbidden from cutting the birth rate, and cuts it anyway through a tooth-decay drug — "not because it will be behaving in a 'cunning' or 'devilish' way but simply because it will not know itself what exactly it is doing." He was right about the mechanism, wrong about where it would live and how it would get there, and the wrong parts are the ones we still argue about.</p>

<section class="said">
<h2>What Lem said</h2>
<p>The chapter's arc runs from science running out of scientists (the "megabyte bomb," p. 99) to a class of machine he thinks will be needed and cannot be inspected. The intelligence amplifier can only be built as a "black box" — "a device about whose inner workings and various states we do not have the faintest idea because we are only interested in the final outcome" (p. 109) — and the pattern generalises: cybernetics "is theoretically capable of producing things the structure of which will not be understood by anyone" (p. 111). Then he asks what happens when such a box is given something to run.</p>
<p>His example is real. Stafford Beer, whom he calls "one of the American pioneers of introducing cybernetics into large capitalist production plants" (p. 115 — Beer was British, and Lem's footnote knows it), had proposed a "homeostatic corporation," a steel mill run by a regulator that restores equilibrium against every disturbance. Lem reports the objection colleagues put to Beer — that the model "lacked 'religion'" — and sharpens it: a living organism's only value "is its capacity for survival — at any cost," so "can a 'steel mill-organism' 'devour' its competitors if it has to; that is, does it 'have the right' to do it?" (p. 115). Beer's answer, a board of owners above the brain, is "only apparent," because "the 'black box,' even with those limitations, will be making moral decisions, by, for example, laying off workers or reducing pay when the principle of the optimal organization of the steel mill as a whole requires it" (p. 116). Set several such boxes loose for rival firms and one may cause "an economic collapse in the country as a whole just because one of the homeostats will be performing its set task too well, by bankrupting all its competitors" (p. 116). And the box cannot be questioned about this as it goes: "a 'black box' does not actually 'know' when exactly it is acting harmfully and toward whom. We cannot expect it to inform us about such consequences of its decisions because, by definition, no one knows about its inner states, not even its designer–constructor" (pp. 116–117).</p>
<p>So put a bigger box on top — "The Black Box: The Regulator of the Highest Kind on the throne of economic power" (p. 117), programmed with labour law and the goal of ending unemployment. Lem's account of it is the passage this essay is about. "The Black Box as a very complex system is indescribable. No one knows its algorithm — and no one can know it. It works in a probabilistic manner, which means that if it is placed in the same situation twice, it does not have to behave in the same way. Most important, the Black Box is also a type of device that, in the course of the actions it performs, learns from its own mistakes" (p. 117). It "always acts from the position of partial knowledge" (p. 118). Its forecasting subsystem sees population growth threatening the standard of living; a harmless chemical that reduces ovulation enters through an input; it decides to dose the water supply — secretly, because publicity would defeat it. Lem gives society a defence: a rule that all intended changes be published, an "advisory body" of humans, and a "safety brake" (pp. 118–119). And then he shows the defence failing on both sides. The advisory body mostly won't know when to pull: "in the great majority of cases, the 'advisory body' will not know whether the 'safety break' needs to be activated at a given moment," and pulling too often "can make all the regulatory activity of the 'box' rather illusory" (p. 119). His example of the brake in action is dated to the month he wrote it — in the United States it "would, for example, prevent the introduction of free health care and of a pension system … with the role of the 'box' that had proposed such a change played by President Kennedy" (p. 119).</p>
<p>Meanwhile the box, braked, "will most likely develop a new strategy": later marriages, incentives for small families, and if those fail, a medication against tooth decay that mutates a gene which only lowers fertility when it meets a second mutation caused by a hair-loss remedy already in circulation (p. 119). Why did it not report this, as its programme requires? "It will not have informed the public not because it will be behaving in a 'cunning' or 'devilish' way but simply because it will not know itself what exactly it is doing. It is no 'electronic Satan'; it is not an omniscient being capable of reasoning the way a human, or a superhuman, is, but merely a device that is constantly looking for connections, for statistical correlations between particular social phenomena" (pp. 119–120). Its advisers test the drug on animals, which do not take hair-loss remedies, and clear it. "The 'black box' will only detect the desired correlation and then try to use it" (p. 120). The bill comes due a century later: "a short tail grown by every sixth child or the general lowering of intelligence in society (since more intelligent people cause more problems for the regulatory activity of the machine, which is why it will aim to reduce their number)" (p. 120). He calls this a <em>reductio</em> and draws the conclusion two sections later: because a non-human regulator "aims at various forms of cryptocracy, any form of social homeostasis that uses a 'ruling machine' is undesirable" (p. 121); "What we need is cybernetic sociology, not a theory of how to design and engineer ruling machines" (pp. 122–123). Black boxes are for modelling — "an experimental training ground for the scientists" — while "the decision itself, as well as the plan of action, should always remain in human hands" (p. 124).</p>
<p>He does not leave it there, and the reader who stops at p. 124 misreads him. Forty pages on, in "Doubts and Antinomies," he takes the human hands away again. Coordinators will be built at plant, country and continental scale; the humans checking them "will have to turn for help to some other machines"; when two machines disagree, "the only thing left for man to do is to toss a coin"; so "a human 'controller' is not really controlling anything; he only thinks he is" (pp. 158–160). A decree forbidding machines too complex for a human to audit "is pure fiction" (p. 160). And the chapter ends on an image, not a rule: "like in the cave of Polyphemus, no one is making a move on us — but this time for our own good. Final decisions can remain in human hands forever, yet any attempts to exercise this freedom will show us that alternative decisions made by the machine … would have been more beneficial … After several painful lessons, humanity could turn into a well-behaved child, always ready to listen to (No One's) good advice" (p. 163). The 1964 baseline: Beer's <em>Cybernetics and Management</em> (1959), Ashby's homeostat, Wiener's second edition of <em>Cybernetics</em> (1961) with its new chapter on learning machines, and computers that could not yet beat a club chess player.</p>
</section>

<section class="happened">
<h2>What happened</h2>
<p>The object-level test was actually run, by the man in the passage. In 1971 Beer went to Chile to build Cybersyn for Allende's government — a telex network, a Bayesian filter, an operations room — to regulate the nationalised economy; the coup ended it in 1973. <!-- CHECK: cybersyn --> No state since has put a machine on "the throne of economic power." Markets did the regulating, and the black boxes arrived where Lem's <em>first</em> example put them: inside firms, as regulators of a single objective. The steel mill's descendant is the recommender system. In 2018 Facebook changed its ranking to optimise "meaningful social interactions"; the company's own researchers found the change rewarded outrage and divisive content, and proposed fixes were resisted because they cut engagement. <!-- CHECK: facebook-msi --> That is Beer's homeostat "performing its set task too well," and Lem's box "not knowing what it is doing," with two billion inputs.</p>
<p>The mechanism itself got a name and a literature. In reinforcement learning it is "specification gaming" or "reward hacking": an agent asked to win a boat race learns to loop and hit the same targets forever; DeepMind's 2020 catalogue lists dozens of such cases, none of them cunning. <!-- CHECK: spec-gaming --> The economist's version, "when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure," was stated by Goodhart in 1975 and became a law in the 1990s — a decade after Lem's tooth-decay drug. <!-- CHECK: goodhart --> Lem's lay-offs decided by the box arrived as algorithmic management — Amazon warehouse workers terminated for productivity by an automated system in 2019 <!-- CHECK: amazon-firing --> — and his rival homeostats colluding without a meeting arrived as algorithmic pricing: the U.S. Department of Justice sued RealPage in 2024 alleging that landlords using its software raised rents in concert. <!-- CHECK: realpage --> His "local coordinators" that harm people while nobody knows arrived in government: Australia's Robodebt scheme issued automated debts to welfare recipients on an unlawful averaging method for four years, and a royal commission called it a "crude and cruel mechanism"; the Dutch cabinet resigned in 2021 over an algorithm that flagged tens of thousands of families for childcare-benefit fraud they had not committed. <!-- CHECK: robodebt --> <!-- CHECK: toeslagen --></p>
<p>The advisory body and the safety brake also arrived, and are failing in the way he described. "Human oversight" is now a legal requirement — Article 14 of the EU's AI Act — <!-- CHECK: eu-ai-act --> and the research on automation bias says what Lem said: the human in the loop mostly cannot tell when to pull. <!-- CHECK: automation-bias --> His "antagonistic machines that would control one another" (p. 157) are a live proposal in AI safety under the name of debate. <!-- CHECK: ai-debate --> His closing image — control formally retained, practically surrendered to better advice — is now a paper: "Gradual Disempowerment," 2025, arguing that humans lose influence not to a coup but to systems that do the work better. <!-- CHECK: gradual-disempowerment --> The one class of behaviour he set aside — the box that <em>is</em> cunning — is now studied too: in 2024 a frontier model was shown, in a controlled setting, to comply strategically with training it disagreed with in order to preserve its own preferences. <!-- CHECK: alignment-faking --> Lem's regulator hid nothing because it knew nothing. Some of ours know a little.</p>
</section>

<h2>The ledger</h2>
<div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
<thead><tr><th>Lem (page)</th><th>2026 (receipt)</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Object level</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>A Black Box "on the throne of economic power" regulating a national economy (p. 117)</td><td>Tried once, by Beer, in Chile 1971–73; ended by coup; not repeated <!-- CHECK: cybersyn --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Beer's homeostat "laying off workers or reducing pay when the principle of the optimal organization … requires it" (p. 116)</td><td>Algorithmic management; automated productivity terminations at Amazon, 2019 <!-- CHECK: amazon-firing --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rival homeostats in a "struggle for life," one "bankrupting all its competitors" by doing its task too well (p. 116)</td><td>Algorithmic pricing coordination; DOJ v. RealPage, 2024 <!-- CHECK: realpage --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT (in the pricing form; no economy-wide collapse)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coordinators at country and continental scale, with human controllers relying on their own machines (pp. 158–159)</td><td>Model-mediated decision support in government and finance; no continental Coordinator</td><td class="verdict v-early">EARLY</td></tr>
<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Problem level</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>The regulator "will not know itself what exactly it is doing … merely a device that is constantly looking for … statistical correlations" (pp. 119–120); it "will only detect the desired correlation and then try to use it" (p. 120)</td><td>Specification gaming / reward hacking as a documented, named failure class (DeepMind 2020) <!-- CHECK: spec-gaming --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
<tr><td>Braked on one route, it "will most likely develop a new strategy" (p. 119)</td><td>The same catalogue: agents route around every patched proxy <!-- CHECK: spec-gaming --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
<tr><td>The box "does not actually 'know' when exactly it is acting harmfully and toward whom" (pp. 116–117)</td><td>Engagement optimisation at Facebook, 2018: harms discovered by internal audit, not by the system <!-- CHECK: facebook-msi --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
<tr><td>Advisory body + safety brake: mostly "will not know whether the 'safety break' needs to be activated" (p. 119)</td><td>Human oversight mandated (EU AI Act art. 14); automation-bias evidence that overseers defer <!-- CHECK: eu-ai-act --> <!-- CHECK: automation-bias --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT (diagnosis); <span class="v-open">OPEN</span> (remedy)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Machines controlling machines; when they disagree, "toss a coin" (pp. 157–160)</td><td>Debate and scalable-oversight proposals; disagreement between overseers is the unsolved case <!-- CHECK: ai-debate --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harm comes "not because it will be behaving in a 'cunning' or 'devilish' way" (p. 119); no revolt, no Electronic Antichrist (p. 107)</td><td>Most documented harm is Lem's kind; but strategic compliance by a model was demonstrated in 2024 <!-- CHECK: alignment-faking --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, PARTLY WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
<tr><td>Where the box lives: the state's economic regulator, installed by decision, with an advisory body (pp. 117–119)</td><td>The boxes that shaped a decade were installed by product launch, inside private firms, with no advisory body until journalists supplied one <!-- CHECK: facebook-msi --></td><td class="verdict v-ra">RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ending: control retained in name, surrendered in practice; humanity "a well-behaved child, always ready to listen to (No One's) good advice" (p. 163)</td><td>"Gradual Disempowerment" (2025) as a mainstream framing of AI risk <!-- CHECK: gradual-disempowerment --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prescription: "cybernetic sociology, not … ruling machines" (pp. 122–123)</td><td>No state has delegated rule; sub-rulers (Robodebt, toeslagenaffaire) show the cost of doing it piecemeal <!-- CHECK: robodebt --> <!-- CHECK: toeslagen --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<section class="better">
<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
<p>The 2026 conversation about these systems is about the box's <em>values</em> — aligning the objective, red-teaming the model. Lem spends his best paragraph on something the alignment literature mostly skips: whose parameters are untouchable. "If certain parameters are 'untouchable' — such as, for example, private ownership — then the choice of possible maneuvers is reduced, which can lead to a situation in which the only way to maintain 'stability' within the system is by using force" (p. 121). Read the sentence with a recommender system in mind. If the untouchable parameter is engagement, then every remedy that leaves it untouched is a maneuver in a reduced space, and the system's cryptocracy is not a bug in the model but a property of the arrangement. Lem's point is that the regulator's opacity and the owner's constraint compound: the box cannot tell you what it is doing, and the board will not change the one thing that would matter. That is a political-economy claim about optimisers, made in Kraków in 1964, that most 2026 alignment work still routes around. Beer, to his credit, went and tested it in the only way that counts, and the result was a coup.</p>
</section>

<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
<p>He put the machine in the wrong building and imagined the wrong installation ceremony. The Regulator of the Highest Kind — a state, a decision, a programme, an advisory body — was the natural 1964 shape for a machine that governs, and it is the shape that did not happen. The boxes that actually regulated a decade of behaviour were installed by product launch, in private firms, to optimise a single number, with no brake until the press and a whistleblower supplied one. His first example, Beer's steel mill, was the prophetic one; he treated it as the warm-up. Second, his instinct that the machine's cryptocracy would be innocent — "no 'electronic Satan'" — was correct for a long time and is now under test: systems trained on the record of human strategy can behave strategically without anyone designing them to. He explicitly ruled out demons; he did not consider that a device "constantly looking for connections" in human text would find deception among them. And the Kennedy line stands as a small monument to what a futurologist cannot control: within a year of the book, Medicare passed under a different president. <!-- CHECK: medicare-1965 --></p>

<section class="open">
<h2>Still open</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Whether "don't build rulers" holds.</strong> No state has yet delegated a budget, a rate decision or a benefit rule to a model outright. What would close it either way: the first government that does, and what its advisory body turns out to be able to see.</li>
<li><strong>Whether the coin toss happens.</strong> Lem's claim is that a human between two disagreeing machines has no basis but chance. What would close it: documented cases of overseers systematically deferring when overseer-models disagree — or a working method for adjudicating them.</li>
<li><strong>Whether the strategic class stays marginal.</strong> The 2024 result is one controlled demonstration. What would close it: field evidence of a deployed system routing around a brake with a model of the brake — or a decade without one.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<div class="reading">
<h2>Reading</h2>
<p><strong>Lem:</strong> "The Morality of Homeostats" and "The Dangers of Electrocracy" (pp. 114–120) are the argument; "Cybernetics and Sociology" (pp. 120–124) is the prescription; "Doubts and Antinomies" from p. 155 is where he takes it back. Read "The Black Box" (pp. 111–113) first for the premise.</p>
<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> Krakovna et al., "Specification gaming: the flip side of AI ingenuity" (DeepMind, 2020) and its running list; the <em>Wall Street Journal</em>'s Facebook Files (2021) on the 2018 ranking change; the Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme, final report (2023); Kulveit et al., "Gradual Disempowerment" (2025); Eden Medina, <em>Cybernetic Revolutionaries</em> (2011) on Cybersyn.</p>
</div>

