     1	# Book 15 — CONTEXT (authority document)
     2	
     3	**Working title: *Sixty Years Later*.** Ten essays reading Stanisław Lem's *Summa
     4	Technologiae* (1964) from 2026: chapter by chapter, where he was right, wrong, right for
     5	the wrong reason, or asking a question that has since dissolved or is still open.
     6	Lem graded himself in "Twenty Years Later" (1982) and again in the 1990s; this book is
     7	the next grading, by a reader who has the things Lem only argued about.
     8	
     9	Precedence (inherited from book8/book12): this file → AGENT.md (reader, priority stack,
    10	verdict rules, pitch gate) → essay contract (§6) → TEMPLATE.md → prose.
    11	
    12	## 1. Thesis
    13	
    14	Lem asked to be judged on *problems*, not gadgets: he wrote that futurology which
    15	predicts devices is astrology, and that the honest job is to map which questions a
    16	technological civilization will be forced to face. So the scoring here is two-layered.
    17	**Layer A — the object level:** did the thing he described come to exist (VR, gene
    18	editing, machine intelligence, information farming, the SETI silence)? **Layer B — the
    19	problem level:** did the *dilemma* he attached to it become the live dilemma, or a
    20	different one, or none? A chapter can score high on B with a zero on A (phantomatics:
    21	headsets are a niche, but the "can you tell?" argument is now a philosophy industry) and
    22	the reverse (his cosmology detail vs the actual Fermi debate). The verdict vocabulary
    23	(§4) exists so the essays never collapse into "prophet / not prophet."
    24	
    25	The book's own claim: Lem's *misses* are the more instructive half. Where he was wrong
    26	he was wrong in a specific, dated way (1960s cybernetics; information theory as the master
    27	science; colloids and ciliates rather than silicon as the substrate of the black box; the
    28	"human-producing" machine judged "incomparably more difficult" than the "travel" one,
    29	p. 206 — the order 2026 actually got) — and 2026's own blind spots are probably of the same
    30	kind. Note for essay 4: he *did* see learning-from-correlation as the black box's mechanism
    31	(pp. 117–120, 153–54); the miss is language as substrate and scale as method, not statistics.
    32	
    33	## 2. Corpus
    34	
    35	- **Primary:** Lem, *Summa Technologiae*, trans. Joanna Zylinska, Minnesota 2013
    36	  (Electronic Mediations 40), 383 pp. Local: `resources/lem-summa-2013.pdf` →
    37	  `resources/text/summa.txt` (both gitignored; rebuild with `scripts/build-corpus.sh`).
    38	  PDF page N == printed page N; the extraction is quotable and section-mapped.
    39	- **Corpus gap (recorded 2026-08-16):** the 2013 translation carries chapters 1–8 only.
    40	  Chapter 9, *Sztuka i technologia*, is absent, as is the 1982 afterword "Dwadzieścia
    41	  lat później" (quoted only in Zylinska's introduction). Essay 9 needs the Polish
    42	  (Wydawnictwo Literackie, 4th exp. ed. 1984 or later) or the Russian (*Сумма
    43	  технологии*, Мир 1968 / АСТ 2002). Until one is on disk, essay 9 quotes cannot be
    44	  gated and the essay does not ship. **Open: user to supply a copy.**
    45	- **Secondary, Lem:** "Twenty Years Later" (written 1982, appended to the 4th ed.), "Thirty Years Later" (1991),
    46	  *Dialogi* (1957), *Filozofia przypadku* (1968) — for how Lem himself re-scored.
    47	- **2026 side:** whatever the essay's claim needs — receipted per claim in
    48	  `checks/claims-2026.tsv`. No claim about "what happened" rides on memory.
    49	
    50	## 3. Spine — ten essays
    51	
    52	| # | Lem chapter (2013 pp.) | Essay working title | Object-level test |
    53	|---|---|---|---|
    54	| 0 | — (Translator's intro; "Twenty Years Later") | How to grade a prophet | the method itself |
    55	| 1 | 1 Dilemmas (24–30) | Is futurology a science yet? | forecasting/x-risk/AI-timeline culture |
    56	| 2 | 2 Two Evolutions (31–58) | The second evolution, observed | Moore's law, tech-as-organism talk, AI as evolutionary process |
    57	| 3 | 3 Civilizations in the Universe (59–92) | Sixty years of silence | 5,000+ exoplanets, no signal; Great Filter, Dark Forest |
    58	| 4 | 4 Intelectronics (93–163) | The black box arrived | LLMs; the "megabyte bomb"; intelligence amplifier; electrocracy |
    59	| 5 | 5 Prolegomena to Omnipotence (164–196) | Design without understanding | ML as imitology; AlphaFold; "methodological madness" |
    60	| 6 | 6 Phantomology (197–236) | Can you tell? | VR's plateau; simulation argument; deepfakes; BCI |
    61	| 7 | 7 The Creation of Worlds (237–293) | Information farming, at last | AI-for-science, automated discovery; linguistic engineering |
    62	| 8 | 8 A Lampoon of Evolution (294–~350) | Redesigning the designer | CRISPR, He Jiankui, longevity, prosthetics; the ESP misstep |
    63	| 9 | 9 Art and Technology (PL/RU only) | The generated image | generative art, music, text — corpus gap, see §2 |
    64	
    65	Order = Lem's order. Essay 0 sets the scoring rules and is written *last* (it must
    66	report the pattern of the other nine, not predict it).
    67	
    68	## 4. Verdict vocabulary (hard rule — every ledger row uses exactly one)
    69	
    70	- **HIT** — described it; it exists/happened substantially as described.
    71	- **MISS** — described it; it did not happen and there is no live path.
    72	- **EARLY** — described it; not yet, but the path is live in 2026 (dated, not vague).
    73	- **RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER** — the dilemma is the live one; his resolution isn't.
    74	- **RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON** — the outcome matches; the mechanism he gave doesn't.
    75	- **DISSOLVED** — the question no longer means anything (its premise fell).
    76	- **OPEN** — 2026 cannot say either way; the essay says why.
    77	
    78	A ledger row is one *specific* Lem claim (quoted, paged) against one *specific* 2026
    79	fact (receipted). "Lem anticipated AI" is not a row. "Lem: an intelligence amplifier will
    80	be a black box whose reasoning cannot be inspected even by its builders (p. 111–113) —
    81	2026: mechanistic interpretability still cannot explain frontier-model outputs (source)"
    82	is a row.
    83	
    84	## 5. Voice and sourcing standard
    85	
    86	- Series voice: plain, argued, no hero-worship. Lem is not a prophet to be vindicated; he
    87	  is a very good interlocutor to be answered. Where he was smug (ESP), say so.
    88	- Lem is always quoted verbatim from the 2013 text with page; paraphrase gets a page too.
    89	  Zylinska's translation choices are noted where they matter (e.g. "intelectronics").
    90	- Every 2026-side factual claim carries `<!-- CHECK: id -->` and a `claims-2026.tsv`
    91	  row with a source. Ships only when the row is not `open`.
    92	- No numbers from memory. Dates, counts, model names, paper titles: source or cut.
    93	- The 1982/1991 self-grades: when Lem already conceded or doubled down, quote him, and
    94	  score 2026 against *that* Lem too. Never grade 1964-Lem on something 1982-Lem fixed
    95	  without saying so.
    96	- Essays are 1,800–3,200 words, on TEMPLATE.md.
    97	
    98	## 6. Essay register (contracts)
    99	
   100	Each essay has one exclusive job; the pitch (AGENT.md) fixes it before drafting.
   101	
   102	- **0 How to grade a prophet.** Job: state and defend the two-layer scoring and the
   103	  vocabulary; report the pattern across essays 1–9 (hit-rate by layer). Not a summary.
   104	- **1 Dilemmas.** Job: Lem's case that technology forecasting is necessary and its
   105	  method (extrapolation of *problems*), against 2026's forecasting culture — Tetlock,
   106	  prediction markets, AI-timeline discourse, EA/x-risk. Question: did futurology become
   107	  what he asked for, or an industry of the astrology he mocked?
   108	- **2 Two Evolutions.** Job: the bio/techno-evolution parallel and his "several naïve
   109	  questions". Test: is technology in 2026 better described as designed or as evolved
   110	  (scaling laws, emergent capabilities, nobody planning the stack)?
   111	- **3 Civilizations.** Job: von Hoerner/Drake-era statistics, his "catastrophic theory",
   112	  votum separatum. Test against exoplanet census, SETI negatives, the modern Fermi
   113	  literature. Where his *specific* numbers went and whether the silence changed meaning.
   114	- **4 Intelectronics.** Job (the book's centre): the megabyte bomb, the black box,
   115	  intelligence amplifiers, "the morality of homeostats", electrocracy, "beliefs of
   116	  electric brains" — against LLMs, alignment, interpretability, AI-in-government. This
   117	  essay must not become a victory lap; the ledger will show what he did *not* foresee
   118	  (learning from data rather than design; language as the substrate).
   119	- **5 Prolegomena.** Job: imitology and "the silence of the designer" — building things
   120	  that work without a theory of why. Test: modern ML *is* this. Where the argument
   121	  about restraint ("Scylla and Charybdis") lands in 2026.
   122	- **6 Phantomology.** Job: peripheral vs central phantomatics, the indistinguishability
   123	  test, cerebromatics, personality-and-information. Test: VR as consumer product (flat),
   124	  simulation argument (Bostrom 2003 onward), deepfakes, BCI, identity/upload debates.
   125	- **7 Creation of Worlds.** Job: information farming — automatic generation of knowledge
   126	  without a scientist — linguistic engineering, engineering of transcendence,
   127	  cosmogonic engineering. Test: AI-driven discovery in 2024–26; LLMs as "linguistic
   128	  engineering"; the last two sections as speculation still.
   129	- **8 Lampoon.** Job: biology as bad engineering, autoevolution, cyborgization,
   130	  constructing consciousness/death — against gene editing, longevity, prosthetics, the
   131	  ethics debates. Also the one section that has aged worst: extrasensory phenomena.
   132	- **9 Art and Technology.** Job: his short closing on art under technology — against
   133	  generative media. Blocked on corpus (§2).
   134	
   135	## 7. Checks
   136	
   137	`./verify.sh` — Lem quotes gating (fixed corpus), 2026 claim markers gating, links and
   138	count-sync gating, structure advisory. `scripts/find.py` writes the quote rows.
   139	`scripts/review.sh N` runs the reviewer panel (AGENT.md §Panel).
   140	
   141	## 8. Status log
   142	
   143	- 2026-08-16 — repo created; corpus extracted (2013 tr., 383 pp); shape scaffolded;
   144	  chapter-9 corpus gap recorded; no essays drafted. Next: pitch essay 4 or 6 first
   145	  (the two with the strongest object-level material) as pilots.
     1	# AGENT.md — instructions for the writing model (book 15)
     2	
     3	## Who is reading
     4	An adult who has heard "Lem predicted everything" and is suspicious of it. They have used
     5	an LLM, may own a headset, read the news about CRISPR babies. They have not read *Summa*
     6	and will not — the essay must carry enough Lem, verbatim, that they don't need to. They
     7	will check a claim if it looks too neat.
     8	
     9	## Priority stack (higher wins on conflict)
    10	1. **Never false.** Lem quoted verbatim, paged, gated. 2026 claims receipted. No numbers
    11	   from memory. Being boring is a failure; being wrong is a betrayal.
    12	2. **Grade, don't celebrate.** Every essay must contain at least one MISS or RIGHT
    13	   QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER, found honestly. If none exists, the essay says why that
    14	   chapter is different — and the panel is told to attack that.
    15	3. **The two layers stay separate.** Object-level and problem-level are scored in
    16	   different ledger rows. "He got the idea" never launders "he got the thing wrong."
    17	4. **The reader must be able to feel 1964.** Give the state of the art Lem was
    18	   extrapolating from (vacuum tubes to transistors, Ashby, Shannon, Wiener, Sputnik-era
    19	   SETI) so the reader can see the distance, not just the endpoint.
    20	
    21	## Verdict rules
    22	- Use CONTEXT §4 vocabulary exactly. One verdict per row. Rows are specific + paged.
    23	- When Lem re-graded himself (1982/1991), score against the later Lem too and say so.
    24	- Distinguish *Lem said* from *Zylinska translated*; check the Polish term when the
    25	  English is doing work (imitology, phantomatics, "intelectronics").
    26	- The "still open" section is not a dumping ground: each open item names what
    27	  evidence would close it.
    28	
    29	## Pitch before writing (the guinea-pig gate)
    30	Never drafted cold. For each essay present the user 2–4 candidate angles, ≤4 sentences
    31	each: the Lem passage that anchors it (paged), the 2026 fact it meets, and the verdict
    32	tension. The user picks; the rest are banked in `drafts/NN.pitches.md`.
    33	
    34	## Pre-ship test (all five or don't ship)
    35	1. Does the essay quote Lem enough that a reader could disagree with *our reading* of
    36	   him from the quotes alone?
    37	2. Is there a real MISS or WRONG-ANSWER row, argued, not token?
    38	3. Is every 2026 claim marked `<!-- CHECK: id -->` with a non-`open` row?
    39	4. Would someone who admires Lem and someone who thinks he's overrated both find the
    40	   ledger fair? (Panel question.)
    41	5. Dinner test: can the reader say in one sentence what Lem got right *and* wrong here,
    42	   without saying "he was ahead of his time"?
    43	
    44	## Panel (review)
    45	`scripts/review.sh N` — grok and agy review independently against
    46	`scripts/prompts/review-checklist.md`; codex consolidates and is adversarial toward
    47	*their* findings. Always check output size (either may return zero bytes). Findings go
    48	to `drafts/reviews/NN-*.md`; the essay is revised, and the correction is logged in
    49	CONTEXT §8.
    50	
    51	## Generation economy
    52	Read the Lem chapter (`scripts/find.py -s "<Section>"`) before pitching. Write the
    53	ledger *before* the prose — the rows are the argument; prose that precedes them
    54	rationalises. Essay 0 last.
