     4	
     5	### 1. The throne is a *reductio*, not a forecast — and the “wrong building” thesis misreads the chapter
     6	
     7	**Location:** “He put the machine in the wrong building”; ledger MISS, “A Black Box ‘on the throne of economic power’ regulating a national economy (p. 117)”; RA/WR, “Where the box lives: the state’s economic regulator, installed by decision.”
     8	
     9	**Problem:** Lem does not predict a state economic ruler. He introduces it with “Let us imagine” after “It might seem that dangers of this kind will be nipped in the bud by constructing a ‘black box’ of a higher type” (p. 117), then closes: “We have thus conducted a *reductio ad absurdum* of the theory of the Black Box: The Regulator of the Highest Kind” (p. 120). The prescription is “undesirable” (p. 121) and “possible yet undesirable” (p. 122). The first worked example is already a private firm — Beer’s steel mill (p. 115). Grading the throne as a failed object-level prophecy, and “wrong building” as what he got worst, scores him for a proposal he was dismantling. Cybersyn tests Beer, not a Lem forecast.
    10	
    11	**Fix:** Drop the throne MISS and the “wrong building” frame. Score the *reductio* as problem-level (cryptocracy / don’t build rulers). If an object-level state-machine row is needed, use the coordinators passage (pp. 158–160), which is the actual forecast.
    12	
    13	---
    14	
    15	### 2. RealPage is the opposite of rival homeostats bankrupting competitors
    16	
    17	**Location:** “his rival homeostats colluding without a meeting arrived as algorithmic pricing”; ledger HIT, “Rival homeostats in a ‘struggle for life,’ one ‘bankrupting all its competitors’… DOJ v. RealPage.”
    18	
    19	**Problem:** Lem’s case is competing homeostats in a “struggle for life,” one “performing its set task too well, by bankrupting all its competitors,” risking “economic collapse in the country as a whole” (p. 116). RealPage is one vendor’s software that landlords feed data into; the DOJ alleged *coordination to raise rents* — a hub, not autonomous rivals destroying each other. Collusion ≠ hyper-competition. The prose even says “colluding,” which concedes the mismatch the HIT then ignores. Hedging “in the pricing form; no economy-wide collapse” does not repair a reversed mechanism.
    20	
    21	**Fix:** Cut the HIT. Use a documented case of independent pricing agents learning to collude, or score p. 116 as MISS / RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER. Facebook MSI already carries “task too well” in the prose; don’t spend this row on a cartel.
    22	
    23	---
    24	
    25	### 3. A 2025 paper is scored as if the Polyphemus ending happened
    26	
    27	**Location:** ledger HIT, “Ending: control retained in name, surrendered in practice… ‘Gradual Disempowerment’ (2025) as a mainstream framing of AI risk”; prose: “His closing image… is now a paper.”
    28	
    29	**Problem:** HIT requires that the thing “exists/happened substantially as described.” Lem described humanity becoming “a well-behaved child, always ready to listen to (No One’s) good advice” (p. 163). A cited arXiv/ICML position paper is not that outcome. “Mainstream framing” is also overstated (one 2025 paper, ~88 citations). This is the checklist’s exact failure: a vague idea credited with a specific 2026 fact that is only *discourse about* the idea.
    30	
    31	**Fix:** EARLY (the path is argued and live) or OPEN. Move “a paper now names this” to prose.
    32	
    33	---
    34	
    35	### 4. Missing MISS the chapter contract required: language as substrate, design vs training
    36	
    37	**Location:** ledger as a whole; “Where he was worst” names building and installation, plus one sentence on deception-in-text.
    38	
    39	**Problem:** CONTEXT §1 and §6: essay 4’s miss is “language as the substrate and scale as method, not statistics”; “the ledger will show what he did *not* foresee (… language as the substrate).” He *did* see correlation-seeking (pp. 119–120) — the essay is right to HIT that. He did not see a box whose material is human text, trained at scale, with no axiomatic kernel of labour law. The constructed homeostat with a programmed “Operating Program” (p. 117) is the object he described; the LLM/ranker is not it. That object-level miss is not a row. Without it the ledger is eight-plus HITs and a disputed MISS — a skeptic’s “he predicted everything” chart.
    40	
    41	**Fix:** Add an object-level row: designed homeostat / programmed axioms / ciliate-colloid construction (pp. 109–117) vs trained-on-language systems. Verdict MISS or RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON (uninspectable optimiser exists; substrate and construction method don’t).
    42	
    43	---
    44	
    45	### 5. Object-level rows contradict each other on the state machine
    46	
    47	**Location:** throne MISS (“ended by coup; not repeated”) next to coordinators EARLY (“Model-mediated decision support… no continental Coordinator”).
    48	
    49	**Problem:** MISS = no live path; EARLY = path is live. Country-scale coordinators (pp. 158–159) *are* the live form of a machine in economic government. If that path is live, the throne cannot be a dead-end MISS. The EARLY 2026 cell is also unmarked (`<!-- CHECK -->` absent) and vague — “decision support in government and finance” is not dated, and is far from Lem’s production/exchange Coordinator.
    50	
    51	**Fix:** One object-level row for state-scale machine regulation, verdict EARLY, with a receipted 2026 instance — or OPEN, and say what would close it. Do not MISS and EARLY the same object.
    52	
    53	---
    54	
    55	### 6. Verdict vocabulary is not used exactly; two verdicts in one row
    56	
    57	**Location:** “HIT (diagnosis); OPEN (remedy)”; “RIGHT QUESTION”; “RIGHT QUESTION, PARTLY WRONG ANSWER”; “HIT (in the pricing form; no economy-wide collapse).”
    58	
    59	**Problem:** CONTEXT §4: exactly one verdict per row, from the closed list. “RIGHT QUESTION” without WRONG ANSWER is not a verdict. “PARTLY WRONG ANSWER” is not a verdict. Two verdicts in one cell is not a row.
    60	
    61	**Fix:** Split oversight into HIT (the advisory body mostly cannot tell) and OPEN (whether a remedy exists). Score the cunning/Antichrist row as RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER *or* HIT on “no revolt,” not a hybrid. Put hedges in prose.
    62	
    63	---
    64	
    65	### 7. “Where the box lives” mixes layers and misuses RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON
    66	
    67	**Location:** problem-level row, “Where the box lives: the state’s economic regulator…”
    68	
    69	**Problem:** Placement is object-level (did the thing exist where he said). RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON = outcome matches, *mechanism* doesn’t. Institution (state vs firm) is not a mechanism. The mechanism rows are already HIT; the throne is already MISS. This row launders the placement error into a problem-level win.
    70	
    71	**Fix:** Delete the row, or rewrite as object-level RA/WR only if the claimed outcome is “uninspectable goal-gamers exist” and the claimed wrong mechanism is “installed by a state with an axiomatic programme” — and then drop the duplicate HITs that already claim that outcome.
    72	
    73	---
    74	
    75	### 8. Amazon productivity firings are not Beer’s black-box homeostat
    76	
    77	**Location:** ledger HIT, “Beer's homeostat ‘laying off workers…’ / Algorithmic management; automated productivity terminations at Amazon, 2019.”
    78	
    79	**Problem:** Lem’s claim is an *indescribable* probabilistic learner making moral allocations when “optimal organization… requires it,” inner states unknown even to the designer (pp. 116–117). Amazon’s 2019 system is a rate threshold that “automatically generates any warnings or terminations… without input from supervisors” (The Verge; Amazon said supervisors can override). Same surface fact (a machine fires people). Not the same object. HIT here is the vague idea (automation of dismissal) credited with the specific box.
    80	
    81	**Fix:** RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON, or move to prose as a cousin. Keep HIT only if the row is narrowed to “a firm optimiser will cut labour when the metric requires it,” and drop the black-box language from the Lem cell.
    82	
    83	---
    84	
    85	### 9. Robodebt / toeslagen are scored as Lem’s coordinators; they are crude, inspectable rules
    86	
    87	**Location:** “His ‘local coordinators’ that harm people while nobody knows arrived in government: Australia’s Robodebt… Dutch cabinet… algorithm that flagged tens of thousands”; OPEN row, “sub-rulers (Robodebt, toeslagenaffaire).”
    88	
    89	**Problem:** Robodebt’s royal commission called it a “crude and cruel mechanism” — unlawful income averaging, not an indescribable box that “does not have to behave in the same way” twice (p. 117). Toeslagen was a risk-classification model plus caseworkers and nationality flags, not a homeostat seeking correlations to game a goal. Both are government automation harm. Neither is Lem’s object. Using them as the 2026 receipt makes the coordinator/electrocracy claim look hit when the matching instance is a spreadsheet with a legal defect.
    90	
    91	**Fix:** If the claim is “automated government harm exists,” say so and don’t call it the Coordinator. If the claim is Lem’s box, these receipts don’t pay it.
    92	
    93	---
    94	
    95	### 10. Facebook MSI is mapped to “the box does not know”; the company knew
    96	
    97	**Location:** “That is Beer’s homeostat ‘performing its set task too well,’ and Lem’s box ‘not knowing what it is doing’”; ledger HIT, “Engagement optimisation at Facebook, 2018: harms discovered by internal audit, not by the system.”
    98	
    99	**Problem:** Lem’s point is constitutive opacity: “no one knows about its inner states, not even its designer–constructor” (pp. 116–117). Facebook researchers identified the outrage incentive and proposed fixes; leadership declined because they cut engagement. That is Lem’s *other* claim — untouchable parameters / reduced maneuver space (p. 121), which the “better than us” section already uses. It is not “the box does not know.” An internal audit that *does* know is the opposite receipt.
   100	
   101	**Fix:** Move Facebook to the p. 121 row (or a new problem-level HIT on untouchable parameters). Do not HIT the opacity sentence with a case whose news value is that humans knew and did not pull.
   102	
   103	---
   104	
   105	### 11. Kennedy/Medicare is graded as a missed forecast; it is a 1964 illustration
   106	
   107	**Location:** “And the Kennedy line stands as a small monument to what a futurologist cannot control: within a year of the book, Medicare passed.”
   108	
   109	**Problem:** Lem writes “In the United States *at the present moment*, it would, for example, prevent…” (p. 119) — present-tense analogy (Kennedy as the “box,” Congress as the brake). He is not predicting that US health insurance will fail. Medicare 1965 does not refute him.
   110	
   111	**Fix:** Cut the monument. If the line is kept, call it a dated example, not a miss.
   112	
   113	---
   114	
   115	### 12. Alignment-faking is used to call his “no demon” answer partly wrong; he hedged
   116	
   117	**Location:** “RIGHT QUESTION, PARTLY WRONG ANSWER”; “He explicitly ruled out demons; he did not consider that a device… in human text would find deception among them.”
   118	
   119	**Problem:** p. 107 rules out revolt and Electronic Antichrist as *anthropomorphic* myths, then hedges: once past a “complication threshold,” machines may show a “personality” unlike ours; “We can expect some surprises, problems, and dangers that we cannot even imagine today.” Alignment-faking (Greenblatt et al. 2024, controlled, one model) is closer to the surprises he allowed than to the Antichrist he denied. Scoring WRONG ANSWER (even “partly”) grades 1964-Lem for a thing he put outside the medieval-demon frame. The text-as-substrate point belongs in finding 4, not as a rebuttal of p. 107.
   120	
   121	**Fix:** HIT or keep OPEN on “no revolt / no Electronic Antichrist.” Put strategic compliance under the language-substrate miss, or OPEN pending field evidence (the still-open bullet already says this).
